    "key": "E",
    "text": "$\\sqrt3$"
   }
  ],
  "answer": "D",
  "acceptedAnswers": null,
  "answerUnit": null,
  "solutions": [
   {
    "title": "30-60-90 triangle",
    "body": "The centres lie on the bisector of angle $R$. Take the right-angled triangle with a vertex at each centre and with sides parallel to $QR$ and $PQ$: it has an angle of $30^\\circ$ at $R$. If the larger radius is $r$, the hypotenuse (joining the centres) is $r+1$ and the shortest side (parallel to $PQ$) is $r-1$. In a $30^\\circ$-$60^\\circ$-$90^\\circ$ triangle the hypotenuse is twice the shortest side: $r+1=2(r-1)$, so $r=3$.\n\n**Answer: (D) 3**"
   }
